On 2009-08-02, ranger <nethack@cox.net> wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> This is not borne out by the source.  A burned-in Elbereth is actually
>> superior to a scroll of Scare Monster, because the effects are identical
>> and an Elbereth cannot be destroyed by ranged fire attacks from a wand
>> or dragon.  (Reflection does not protect items on the ground.)
> Yes, but my point is that if I come across a ?oSM early in the game it 
> can be a significant game changer.  If I find a wand of lightning or 
> fire, that will also, most definitely change the balance of power.

The former is far less of a game-changer than the latter.  The ?oSM is
only effective as a perpetual resource when it can be repeatedly
re-blessed; otherwise it is one-use and de facto one-level.  No single
level in the early game is worth very much in particular except for
Minetown, and even that has the handicap of the creatures you _really_
want to kill don't respect the scroll anyway.  Consider what you're
positing here:

a) You find a ?oSM early in the game.
b) You find a way to concretely identify the scroll.
b2) Alternately, you find two scrolls and read-ID the first.
b3) Alternately, you find the scroll on the Minetown ground.
c) You successfully get the scroll to Minetown and drop it or kick it to...
somewhere, without getting it stuck against a shop wall or in a corner.
d) ...yeah, I still don't see the game-changing aspect here, sorry.

The player needs to be moving through the game, not parked in a single
spot afraid to leave his scroll.  The wands of fire and lightning allow
that to happen, as they can explore _with_ the player and provide him
protection in places he could need it most (take, for example, the
doorway to the Sokoban zoo?  Normally the critters in it aren't so bad,
but if you've built up in levels or are light on armor and resources, a
single burned E can let you clean the thing out and get the prize.  You
won't be kicking any scroll of SM down _that_ hallway.)

> I consider every resource scarce enough that it's worth maximizing its 
> value.  It's not a huge deal, but a ?oSM that I can kick to a strategic 
> location is being put to better use for a safe square than a crumbled  ID.

Resources in Nethack are far from limited.  Consider what you're saying
here; you'd rather spend a great deal of effort ensuring that you can
get the full '3' out of a '3'-grade resource, instead of not worrying
about it, moving through the dungeon, and focusing on getting some of 
the '7'- or '8'- grade resources out there instead.

I'm not saying don't use the scroll, now.  I'm just saying that spending
so much effort to avoid blowing one up just isn't that useful, because
the scroll _itself_ isn't that useful.  The more complex any plan, the
more points it has to fail, and a complex plan that doesn't even come
out with significant positive at the end... yeah, that just doesn't make
sense.

